INT. CAPE ROSS TELEGRAPH OFFICE - NIGHT - 1889

A stone hut at the edge of a cliff. A potbellied stove, a battered desk, a brass key under a green-shaded lamp. Wind howls around the shutters. A storm lantern swings from a hook.

CLARENCE PENHALLOW, 40s, a telegraph operator in sleeve garters and a worn waistcoat, taps the key steadily, one eye on a log book. Beside him on a stool sits MABEL PENHALLOW, 10, his daughter, bundled in a shawl and chewing a pencil, copying out letters in a copybook.

CLARENCE
(muttering)
Dash-dot-dash... dash-dash...

MABEL
What does it say, Papa?

CLARENCE
Ship in distress. Off the Skerries.

MABEL
Is she sinking?

CLARENCE
She's calling.

A bell rings above the door. Clarence picks up the telephone receiver, wired to a grey box on the wall, and holds it to his ear.

HOLLINGSWORTH (V.O.)
(over telephone, crackling)
Penhallow. This is Hollingsworth at Lloyd's. Do you read me?

CLARENCE
I read you, sir.

HOLLINGSWORTH (V.O.)
(over telephone)
The Admiralty has authorized the lifeboat. They want the coordinates relayed to Falmouth. Immediately.

CLARENCE
Coordinates, sir. Fifty degrees, four minutes north. Five degrees, nineteen west.

HOLLINGSWORTH (V.O.)
(over telephone)
Repeat.

CLARENCE
Fifty, four, north. Five, nineteen, west.

A pause. Static.

HOLLINGSWORTH (V.O.)
(over telephone)
Good man. Stay on the wire.

Clarence hangs up and begins to tap at the key, fast, sure, fingers dancing. Mabel watches, wide-eyed. The wind howls. The lantern sways.

MABEL
Papa, will they be in time?

CLARENCE
(without stopping)
Not if I stop to talk.

Mabel nods. Bends over her copybook. The key chatters on into the dark.
